+# Contributing
+
+Thanks for improving this awesome list.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+- Every submission must be a public GitHub repository with a `github.com` URL.
+- No proprietary SaaS listings are allowed.
+- The project must be meaningfully related to open source AI automation.
+- Include the repository license, primary language, and a short explanation of the automation use case.
+- Prefer projects with active maintenance, clear documentation, and reproducible setup instructions.
+
+## How To Submit
+
+1. Fork the repository.
+2. Add the new tool in the most relevant category in `README.md`.
+3. Use this format:
+
+```md
+#### [Tool Name](https://github.com/username/repo)
+> **Description:** Short README-based summary with what it does, key features, license, stars, language, and activity details.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/username/repo](https://github.com/username/repo)
+- **Stars:** 123 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** YYYY-MM-DD
+- **Category:** tag1, tag2
+- **Best for:** specific use case
+```
+
+4. Open a pull request with links to the GitHub repo and supporting evidence for the metadata you added.
+
+## Quality Bar
+
+- Keep descriptions specific and useful.
+- Do not add duplicate repositories.
+- If a category has fewer than 5 good repositories, document that gap honestly instead of padding the list.
+- If metadata is uncertain, say so in the pull request and point to the GitHub source.
